  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 134 AM CST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nThe aforementioned mid-level trough over MX increasing \nwest/northwest winds over the area will assist in mixing stronger \nwinds down to the surface and aiding downslope warming on \nSaturday. This will be aided by continued near surface lee \ntroughing. Unlike this week, winds aloft and winds near the \nsurface will be more aligned directionally, enhancing westerly \nwinds near the surface. Grids still shows winds from the west of \nat least 25 to 35 mph for foothills and plains leeward of western \nhigher terrain, and up to 40 mph for the Guadalupes/Delawares. \nAdditionally, ensemble probabilities of gusts up to 50 mph over \nthe usual windier locations of the Guadalupes/Delawares still \nremain below 30% and too short in duration (<3 hours) to warrant \nhigh wind products at this time - also supported by NWP guidance. \nDownslope warming nevertheless means highs soaring to 70F-80F, \n60F-70F for higher elevations of the Guadalupes down into the \nMarfa Plateau. 80F-85F highs in warmest locations also cannot be \nruled out. In spite of the warm daytime temperatures and winds \nstill gusting to 20 to 30 mph Saturday night, very low boundary \nlayer moisture characterized by dew point temperatures below 30F \nwill be present following the cold snap early this week, and winds\nare expected to shift to from the west to northwest Saturday \nnight as a result of a rapidly forming Pacific cold front \ndeveloping in from the northwest. The combination of CAA and \nefficient radiational cooling is forecast to drop lows to 35F- 40F\nfor northern portions of the area and highest elevations, with \n40F-50F readings for the lowlands.\n\nThe relatively sudden change to cooler weather will be short- \nlived. Continued CAA Sunday keeps temperatures similar to \nThanksgiving Day (highs below 60F northeast of the southern Rio \nGrande basin, below 50F for highest elevations and low-lying \nbasins), with gusty 20 to 30 mph winds from the northwest \npersisting for the Rio Grande basins during the afternoon before \ndiminishing as overnight lows drop into the 35F-40F range \nareawide. However, by early next week, ensembles depict a long \nwave mid-level ridging and quasi zonal air pattern developing over\nthe western CONUS. Consequently, highs rise back into the 60F-75F\nrange for most of the area Monday, and into the 65F- 75F range, \n75F-85F for warmer locations next Tuesday and Wednesday. Continued\nvery low boundary layer moisture maintains at least a 20F- 25F \ndiurnal range and lows still largely in the 45F-55F range. This \nwill translate to cool but less chilly mornings compared to this \nweek, even as we reach and then pass the meteorological start of \nwinter by December 1. The prevailing sinking motion aloft from the\nridging pattern will continue to mean near zero rain chances \nafter this early week's brush with wintry precipitation.",
